---
const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Who reads llms.txt?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "AI tools and agents that use it for curated context: it points models to your best pages in Markdown. Adoption is growing across AI products and agent frameworks." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Does llms.txt improve AI citations?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "It helps models find your canonical pages quickly, which improves the odds of accurate citations, but it is not a guarantee: content quality and crawler access still decide." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Is llms.txt required?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "No. It is a strong recommendation for AI-facing sites. Missing llms.txt is scored in our AI readiness check as a gap, not an error." } }
  ],
};
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

---
<BaseLayout schema={[faqSchema]} title="What Is llms.txt? The AI Content Map Explained" description="llms.txt explained: the proposed markdown standard that helps language models understand your site, how it differs from robots.txt, and how to publish one."  
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["AI Web Infrastructure", "/ai-web-infrastructure/"], ["What Is llms.txt"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/ai-web-infrastructure/" class="hover:text-accent">AI Web Infrastructure</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">What Is llms.txt</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">What Is llms.txt?</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      llms.txt is a proposed standard: a markdown file at your site root that gives language models
      a curated map of what your content is and where the important pages live. Think of it as an
      abstract and table of contents written for machines that read your whole site anyway.
    </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">llms.txt vs robots.txt</h2>
    <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>robots.txt</th><th>llms.txt</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Controls</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Crawling</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Curation</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Audience</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">All crawlers</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">AI models</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Format</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Directives</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Markdown</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#the-problem-it-solves">The problem it solves</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-it-looks-like">What it looks like</a></li>
        <li><a href="#llmstxt-vs-robotstxt">llms.txt vs robots.txt</a></li>
        <li><a href="#publish-yours-in-two-minutes">Publish yours in two minutes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="the-problem-it-solves">The problem it solves</h2>
      <p>
        Models and AI agents visiting your site today crawl pages the way a stranger walks into a
        building: everything is technically visible, nothing is labeled, and the important rooms look
        identical to the boilerplate. llms.txt states the thesis of your site in one blockquote line
        and then lists the pages that matter, each with a one-sentence description. Retrieval systems
        and agents that support the convention get a clean entry point; those that don't simply
        ignore the file, so there is no downside to publishing one.
      </p>
      <h2 id="what-it-looks-like">What it looks like</h2>
      <pre class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft"># PromptDNS

> Free DNS, email authentication, and domain health tools with guides for every record type.

## Tools
- [DNS Propagation Checker](https://promptdns.com/dns-propagation-checker/): check changes across public resolvers.
- [SPF Flattener](https://promptdns.com/spf-flattener/): fix the 10-lookup limit free.

## Guides
- [What Is DNS?](https://promptdns.com/what-is-dns/): the Domain Name System explained.</pre>
      <h2 id="llmstxt-vs-robotstxt">llms.txt vs robots.txt</h2>
      <p>
        Opposite jobs. <a href="/robots-txt-guide/">robots.txt</a> restricts: which crawlers may fetch
        what. llms.txt explains: what this site is and what is worth reading. You need both thinking
        behind them, but they never conflict: one gates access, the other guides comprehension.
      </p>
      <h2 id="publish-yours-in-two-minutes">Publish yours in two minutes</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/llms-txt-generator/">free llms.txt generator</a> builds the file from your site
        name, a one-line summary, and your link list (it can even fetch your title and description
        automatically), then hands you copy-paste or download output. Verify the result with the
        <a href="/ai-readiness-checker/">AI readiness audit</a>.
      </p>
      
<h2 id="related">Related</h2>
